{
  "anomaly_detected": true,
  "confidence": 0.98,
  "anomalies": [
    {
      "category": "polarity",
      "components": ["Q5", "U42"],
      "pins": ["Q5.1", "Q5.2", "Q5.3", "U42.1", "U42.6"],
      "nets": ["+5V", "NET0192"],
      "observed": "Q5's source (pin 2) is on the system +5V rail, while its drain (pin 3) is on NET0192, the USB-C VBUS net. U42's VIN pin is also on NET0192 and SENSE is on +5V. The PMOS is therefore oriented opposite the usual LTC4412 ideal-diode input-to-load arrangement.",
      "expected": "For the intended USB input ideal-diode path, Q5 should have its source toward USB VBUS (U42 VIN) and its drain toward the +5V load rail (U42 SENSE), providing forward input power and reverse-current blocking.",
      "evidence": [
        {
          "source": "netlist",
          "quote": "(net (name \"+5V\") ... (node (ref \"Q5\") (pin \"2\") (pinfunction \"S\")))"
        },
        {
          "source": "netlist",
          "quote": "(net (name \"NET0192\") ... (node (ref \"Q5\") (pin \"3\") (pinfunction \"D\")) ... (node (ref \"U42\") (pin \"1\") (pinfunction \"VIN\")))"
        },
        {
          "source": "netlist",
          "quote": "(net (name \"+5V\") ... (node (ref \"U42\") (pin \"6\") (pinfunction \"SENSE\")))"
        },
        {
          "source": "spec",
          "quote": "\"USB power-path controller intended to OR/protect a USB power input\""
        },
        {
          "source": "spec",
          "quote": "\"need reverse current protection mostly\""
        }
      ],
      "impact": "The reversed external PMOS can prevent the USB input from correctly feeding the system rail and undermines reverse-current blocking; the intended USB power-path behavior may fail or allow the system rail to backfeed USB VBUS.",
      "confidence": 0.97
    },
    {
      "category": "bridged_nets",
      "components": ["U18", "U48"],
      "pins": ["U18.4", "U18.5", "U48.4", "U48.5"],
      "nets": ["GND"],
      "observed": "Both ground pins of each isolated CAN transceiver are tied to the same GND net: U18 GND1/GND2 and U48 GND1/GND2.",
      "expected": "The logic-side and bus-side grounds of each isolated CAN interface should remain galvanically separate for the specified isolated CAN buses.",
      "evidence": [
        {
          "source": "netlist",
          "quote": "(net (name \"GND\") ... (node (ref \"U18\") (pin \"4\") (pinfunction \"GND1\")) (node (ref \"U18\") (pin \"5\") (pinfunction \"GND2\")))"
        },
        {
          "source": "netlist",
          "quote": "(net (name \"GND\") ... (node (ref \"U48\") (pin \"4\") (pinfunction \"GND1\")) (node (ref \"U48\") (pin \"5\") (pinfunction \"GND2\")))"
        },
        {
          "source": "spec",
          "quote": "\"two isolated CAN buses (CAN0, CAN1)\""
        }
      ],
      "impact": "Connecting both sides of each isolation barrier to the same ground defeats galvanic isolation, allowing ground-potential differences and common-mode disturbances on the CAN cabling to couple into the motherboard.",
      "confidence": 0.99
    }
  ],
  "summary": "The netlist shows two supported deviations: the USB ideal-diode PMOS is oriented opposite the input-to-load path, and both CAN transceivers have their isolated-side grounds tied to logic ground. These compromise the intended USB power protection and CAN galvanic isolation."
}

